{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>You shall surely set a king over yourself, etc.</b> Many ask who will show us good — [seeking] a sufficient explanation for the great question that falls upon this commandment: for if it were the will of HaShem that a king should reign over them of justice whenever they so desire, then why did the prophet Samuel, with HaShem with him, become displeased with them when they asked for themselves a king — implying that the matter was abominable in the eyes of HaShem? And some say that the indignation was because they said, 'To judge us like all the nations' — that he should judge them by the laws of the nations. And some say that this passage is not a commandment at all, but a foretelling of things, the Holy One, blessed be He, declaring from the beginning the end: that thus Israel would ask in the future, and HaShem permitted them. And Maharai Abravanel elaborated at length on this question." "And I say my opinion in brief: the divine will was that a king of justice should reign over them in order to cast his awe upon all of them, as the Sages, of blessed memory, said (Avot 3:2): 'Pray for the welfare of the government, for were it not for the fear of it, etc.' And not for the purpose of judgments, for behold, courts were established in every city; but rather for the purpose of assembly and the ordering of the state. But only in this manner — that the fear of the kingdom be upon them — for in this HaShem delighted. And so He said to Joshua (Deuteronomy 31:23): 'For you shall bring this people' — take a staff and strike them on their heads. Therefore it says: 'When you come into the land,' etc. — for after inheritance and settlement, Jeshurun grew fat and kicked, and each man did what was right in his own eyes when there was no king in Israel; and then surely the survivors whom HaShem calls will say that it is time to act, to ask for a king: 'And you shall say: I shall set over myself a king' — that his awe be upon me, for thus the expression 'over myself' implies, that he be supreme over me and rule and dominate me with a strong hand; 'like all the nations that are around me' — to do according to their established practices, who do not kick against their kings and accept authority upon themselves. And the condition of our times is proof enough. And concerning this it is said, 'You shall surely set over yourself a king' — for this request is proper when there is a king 'over yourself,' that is, his awe upon you, as is understood from the word 'over yourself.'" "But in the days of Samuel they did not speak correctly, for they said, 'Give us a king' (I Samuel 8:6) — they did not say 'a king over us' but rather 'to us.' For they did not wish to accept authority such that his awe would be upon them; rather, he should be delivered into our hands, to install kings and depose kings. And what emerges for us from this is that against his will he will flatter us. Therefore they said: 'To judge us like all the nations' (ibid. 8:5), for the primary need for flattery is in matters of judgment — like the custom of those lands that elect a certain magistrate for a fixed term so that his awe not be upon them, and on the contrary, their awe upon the magistrate; and therefore he is compelled to flatter them so that they not reject him. 'And the matter was displeasing in the eyes of Samuel' when they said, 'Give us a king' — for the principal indictment lay in the words 'to us, to judge us': for if he is 'to us' and not 'over us,' how will he judge us? 'And HaShem said to Samuel: Hearken to their voice, etc. for it is not you they have rejected' (ibid. 8:7) — for on the contrary, this is your praise, for you are regarded among them as one who does not flatter them; therefore they sought in your place a king who would flatter them of necessity. And in this they rejected Me, for they did not ask according to the Torah, for their entire desire was that the king nullify My will before their will. And this is what He said, 'from reigning over them' — meaning, in this they rejected Me, that they did not want the king to reign over them. And now, hearken to their voice: 'Only you shall solemnly warn them and declare to them the manner of the king who shall reign over them' (ibid. 8:9) — specifically 'over them' and not 'to them,' for in these things I have desired, says HaShem. 'And he said: This shall be the manner of the king who shall reign over you' (ibid. 8:11) — for even if you did not choose him except for yourselves, in any event he will ultimately reign over you with poured-out fury, and 'he will take your sons,' etc.; and this is your punishment for not having asked as is proper. 'And you shall cry out on that day because of your king whom you have chosen for yourselves' (ibid. 8:18) — meaning, and not 'over you'; and you shall say, by way of confession, that the opposite has befallen you from what you sought. 'But the people refused, etc. and said: No, but a king shall be over us' (ibid. 8:19) — what they said, 'No,' was by way of confession, as if to say: We no longer desire a king in the manner that we first asked, saying 'Give us a king'; 'but a king shall be over us' — as HaShem spoke. And so it says afterward: 'For we have added to our sins to ask for ourselves a king' (ibid. 12:19) — and they meant: at the beginning, before we heard the punishment. And this interpretation is precious. And the change of expression between 'over us' and 'to us' is self-evident, and from the side of the precise reading of the Sages, of blessed memory, who said that his awe must be upon you (Ketuvot 17a) — for the essence of the commandment depends on this." "And as for what He said, 'And HaShem said to Samuel: Hearken to their voice, etc. and you shall appoint for them a king' (I Samuel 8:22) — and He should have said 'over them' — [this is] because the acceptance of fear depends on those who receive it, and HaShem did not bring this about; rather, He gave them a king who was equivalent for them, one whose heart was not raised above his brothers. Therefore at the time of action He said 'for them'; but in any event, for Israel it would not have been proper to say thus, but only according to the word of HaShem, who said, 'And you shall say: I shall set over myself a king' — thus they should have said."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:41.353746211Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}